From 4b5b05d549c439bdfe04156dcaff04bb255e49e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 16:21:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] utils/pty: let an observer see a PTY child's exit before its
 reap

Downstream patch by the CodeSpace project (novelKR/CodeSpace) on
rust-v0.154.0 (6b9826e3aa83b1a5947db50f4332cb9c65f1b340). Not part of
upstream Codex. It ports the generic observer of CodeSpace's W3
experiment B (a scratch patch on rust-v0.159.0-alpha.11, see DevGuard's
docs/handoff/2026-09-28-w3-decision-packet.md, section 3.2) to both Unix
PTY paths of this release, Linux included, without the experiment's
trace instrumentation.

Both Unix PTY spawn paths reap the child in their waiter right after
spawn, so a caller cannot look at what the exited child left behind
(for example descendants still in its process group) while the child's
PID and process group ID are still held.

spawn_pty_process_observed takes an observe::ExitObserver, registered
before the spawn. The child's single waiter then waits for the exit
without consuming it (waitid with WNOWAIT), publishes one
ExitOpportunity that carries no PID, waits until the observer
acknowledges it or the observer's bound passes, and only then reaps
through its original child object. After the reap it reports how the
observation ended (Completed, TimedOut, Failed, Unavailable); an
acknowledgement after the bound returns OpportunityExpired. A dropped
opportunity, or an observer that stopped listening, ends the wait at
once. Termination and Drop in the window still reach the process group,
which the unreaped child keeps, and the child is reaped once.
spawn_pty_process and every other path are unchanged. Other platforms
refuse an observed spawn before creating a process.

Tests, on both Unix paths: the observer sees the exited child unreaped
and the exit code arrives after the acknowledgement; the bound reaps
without one and refuses a late one; a dropped opportunity or observer
does not hold the child; a descendant that ignores SIGHUP is still in
the root's process group during the window; termination in the window
kills the group and the child is reaped once; a handle dropped in the
window leaves the reap to the waiter, after the acknowledgement; output
written before the exit drains in full; a runtime shutdown in the
window waits only for the bound.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
